# DURABLE REPRO — `&`-of-a-projection in an OPERAND position DUPLICATES the
# user `Drop`. Same D4/D12 single-owner violation the array-literal costume has,
# in a position no filed census has a subject for.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, C backend): the program prints
#     1
#     close 9
#     close 9        <- the owner's `drop` runs TWICE
# while the CONTROL — the identical program with the sigil removed
# (`if p.fd > 5:`) — correctly prints `1` then a single `close 9`.
#
# So `&` ALONE is the cause. Nothing else differs between the two programs.
#
# ROOT (shared with the already-documented array-literal costume):
# `reject_tainted_formation_arg` is wired at only TWO call sites,
# `src/semantic/safety/check_expr.rs:116` and `:757`, and BOTH are `CallArg`
# positions. So the 2T reject never sees a non-arg formation — and the
# characterisation "literal-element positions escape 2T" UNDERCOUNTS it:
# **every non-`CallArg` position escapes**, including every operand position.
#
# INTENDED: the owner's `drop` runs exactly ONCE — `1` then a single `close 9`
# — or the program is REJECTED at `gg check` (the likely reference-grade answer:
# an operand is a read, so a `&` there is meaningless). Running a user's
# destructor twice is not a defensible third option; it is the double-free class
# in user-visible form.
#
# ⚠ `fd` is deliberately an `int` so the duplication is OBSERVABLE as two prints
# rather than only as a heap error. A heap payload would additionally trip ASan;
# the print form is what makes this assertable in the normal suite.
#
# ⚠ ggdef cannot adjudicate the operand family (out of phase-0 subset), so
# C/LLVM agreement is necessary-not-sufficient here (Core #8).
#
# Cited from TODO.md: the operand-position `&`-formation family.

struct FH:
    int fd

equip FH with Drop:
    void drop(!self):
        print(f"close {self.fd}")

int seed():
    return 9

void pokep(FH p):
    if &p.fd > 5:
        print(1)

void main():
    FH h = FH(seed())
    pokep(h)
